      {
        "id": "in-style",
        "type": "input.markdown",
        "title": "Reporting House Style",
        "data": {
          "filename": "reporting-house-style.md",
          "instruction": "House style for music-store analytics reporting:\n- Lead with the headline number, then the comparison window.\n- Revenue by genre, revenue by country, top customers and invoice trends each get their own section.\n- Cite tables for every figure. Round money to whole USD.\n- Close with exactly three recommended actions."
        },
        "position": {
          "x": 40,
          "y": 80
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "router1",
        "type": "route.classifier",
        "data": {
          "branches": [
            {
              "id": "b-report",
              "name": "full_report"
            },
            {
              "id": "b-metric",
              "name": "quick_metric"
            },
            {
              "id": "b-deep",
              "name": "database_deep_dive"
            },
            {
              "id": "b-context",
              "name": "sql_specialist"
            },
            {
              "id": "b-chat",
              "name": "conversation"
            }
          ],
          "fallback": "conversation",
          "rules": "Check this first, before anything else: if the message refers back to an answer already given - 'how did you get that', 'why', 'explain that', 'what does that mean', 'where is that from' - it is ALWAYS conversation, no matter what the earlier answer was about. It asks about an answer, not for data.\n\nOtherwise classify by what the user wants DONE WITH the numbers, not by which numbers they name. full_report: the user wants the whole store-analytics report produced and SENT - this branch does not answer in the chat, it drafts the report, stops for a human to approve it, and emails it. Only pick it for an explicit ask for the report as a deliverable ('the full store analytics report', 'email me the report', 'send the report to the team'). Naming several metrics is NOT this branch, however many are named. quick_metric: one specific number or comparison ('how much revenue did Rock earn', 'how many invoices are there'). database_deep_dive: the user wants to SEE several numbers, a breakdown, or a trend over time - more than one query's worth, asked as a question to answer here. 'Show trends', 'monthly sales and media-type mix', 'top genres plus revenue by country' are all this branch. sql_specialist: one precise, deep SQL question - an exact query, a tricky JOIN, or schema detail - best answered by the focused NL-to-SQL workflow. conversation: greetings, follow-ups about a previous answer (how did you get this, explain, why), questions about what this workflow can do, and anything that fits no other branch."
        },
        "position": {
          "x": 360,
          "y": 380
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "lead1",
        "type": "orchestrate.supervisor",
        "title": "Analytics Lead",
        "data": {
          "maxSubtasks": 4,
          "instruction": "Produce the Chinook music-store analytics report. Split into per-specialist subtasks: sales (revenue totals, revenue by genre, top tracks and albums - tables Invoice, InvoiceLine, Track, Album, Genre), customers (top customers by spend, revenue by billing country - tables Customer, Invoice), trends (invoice totals over time, media-type mix, purchase falloff from tracks browsed to tracks bought - tables Invoice, InvoiceLine, MediaType, Track). Each subtask names the tables to use."
        },
        "position": {
          "x": 700,
          "y": 380
        }
